        "content": "TOPIC: Object-Oriented Programming\nSECTION: Object-Oriented Programming — Practical Development Workflow\n\nTOPIC TECHNICAL FOUNDATION\nJava object-oriented design uses classes, objects, encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance, polymorphism, interfaces and composition. Correct design protects invariants, limits mutable state, separates responsibilities and favours clear contracts. Composition and interface-based design often reduce coupling compared with deep inheritance hierarchies.\n\nSECTION EXPLANATION\nGive a repeatable workflow covering source changes, dependency checks, compilation, focused testing, packaging and review. Explain the purpose of each step and the evidence of success.\n\nENGINEERING IMPLEMENTATION\nApply this concept using the standard Java or Spring Boot mechanisms relevant to the topic. Keep responsibilities explicit, validate external inputs, use appropriate types and APIs, handle errors without hiding their causes, and document important configuration decisions. Avoid unnecessary dependencies and verify compatibility with the project's JDK and framework versions.\n\nDEVELOPMENT AND VERIFICATION\nInspect the source and configuration involved. Implement the change in a focused manner. Run the relevant unit and integration tests. For Maven projects, use:\nmvn clean test\nmvn package\n\nReview compiler output, test reports and generated artifacts. A successful build alone does not prove correct runtime behaviour; execute the relevant application path and verify its observable result.\n\nFAILURE HANDLING\nWhen behaviour differs from expectations, reproduce the issue, capture the exact exception and stack trace, inspect logs and runtime configuration, isolate the responsible component and add a regression test. Preserve the original cause when reporting errors. Never expose passwords, tokens or sensitive configuration in logs.\n\nPRODUCTION ENGINEERING\nIntegrate the implementation with controlled configuration, dependency management, automated testing, artifact versioning, deployment checks and operational monitoring. Define health indicators and recovery steps appropriate to the application. Verify normal operation and failure behaviour after deployment.\n\nACCEPTANCE CRITERIA\nThe implementation must preserve the project's existing contracts, pass relevant automated tests, behave predictably for invalid and boundary conditions, provide actionable diagnostics and remain maintainable by another engineer."
