        "title": "Collections and Generics — Language and Runtime Fundamentals",
        "content": "TOPIC: Collections and Generics\nSECTION: Collections and Generics — Language and Runtime Fundamentals\n\nTOPIC TECHNICAL FOUNDATION\nThe Java Collections Framework includes List, Set, Queue, Deque and Map abstractions. ArrayList, LinkedList, HashSet, TreeSet, HashMap and TreeMap have different ordering, lookup, mutation and memory characteristics. Generics provide compile-time type safety; equals and hashCode contracts are essential for hash-based collections.\n\nSECTION EXPLANATION\nDefine the fundamental language or runtime contract. Explain its purpose, terminology, where it is used, compile-time versus runtime behaviour, and how it participates in a Java application.\n\nENGINEERING IMPLEMENTATION\nApply this concept using the standard Java or Spring Boot mechanisms relevant to the topic. Keep responsibilities explicit, validate external inputs, use appropriate types and APIs, handle errors without hiding their causes, and document important configuration decisions. Avoid unnecessary dependencies and verify compatibility with the project's JDK and framework versions.\n\nDEVELOPMENT AND VERIFICATION\nInspect the source and configuration involved. Implement the change in a focused manner. Run the relevant unit and integration tests. For Maven projects, use:\nmvn clean test\nmvn package\n\nReview compiler output, test reports and generated artifacts. A successful build alone does not prove correct runtime behaviour; execute the relevant application path and verify its observable result.\n\nFAILURE HANDLING\nWhen behaviour differs from expectations, reproduce the issue, capture the exact exception and stack trace, inspect logs and runtime configuration, isolate the responsible component and add a regression test. Preserve the original cause when reporting errors. Never expose passwords, tokens or sensitive configuration in logs.\n\nPRODUCTION ENGINEERING\nIntegrate the implementation with controlled configuration, dependency management, automated